> “In the new era, thought itself will be transmitted by radio.” - Guglielmo Marconi

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/issa-01.png" alt="Line drawing of a radio studio floor plan, with a host seated at a round table and a second figure at a desk in the adjoining control room" /%}

*Traditional FM/AM Networks: The inception of radio broadcasting.*

Canadian-born inventor Reginald Fessenden is widely credited with inventing AM (amplitude modulation) radio. While his contemporary, Guglielmo Marconi, was successfully transmitting wireless Morse code, Fessenden focused on the challenge of transmitting the human voice. On Christmas Eve in 1906, he made history by broadcasting the first-ever radio program of voice and music from Brant Rock, Massachusetts, which was picked up by ships far out at sea.

Edwin Howard Armstrong invented FM (frequency modulation) radio in 1933 to solve the static and interference problems plaguing early audio transmissions. To make FM radio work, Armstrong utilized wide-band frequency modulation, which edited the signal's frequency rather than its amplitude. Back then, FM radio was primarily a revolutionary, high-fidelity experimental technology used for high-quality musical broadcasts and military communications during World War II due to its resistance to static.

The buildings where early radio was broadcast from included not only a studio, but a power room, a control room, and a music studio. The architectural layout originally included these rooms to house a variety of generators, equipment, and of course, a live audience. Ornate, wooden wardrobes, chandeliers, velvet curtains, and detailed, seemingly persian rugs decorated the space. A grand piano almost always took up a corner in the music studio. Male hosts dressed in suits, while female hosts wore fancy dresses with large skirts, despite the fact that most of the audience could not look at the performers. In the 50s, studios within studios began occurring, some headquarters of Radio Stations with up to five studios, each with their own control rooms.

